WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.378
A café owner phones support.

2
00:00:04.378 --> 00:00:07.193
“Show me my failed payments from this morning.”

3
00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:12.941
Last week, on a database on one machine, the same question came back in a few milliseconds.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:22.164
This week the data lives in a new store spread over 40 machines, and the screen shows an error: this query “might involve data filtering”.

5
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:27.459
That's the store's polite way of saying: I'd have to read everything, and I'd rather not.

6
00:00:27.459 --> 00:00:30.453
So why would a bigger store refuse a small question?

7
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:32.141
Think of a supermarket.

8
00:00:32.141 --> 00:00:38.433
Its shelves are arranged by kind, so all the pasta is in one aisle, and all the tins in another.

9
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:41.183
Ask for pasta, and you walk to one aisle.

10
00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:48.073
Ask for everything on special offer, and you walk every aisle, because the shelves aren't arranged by offer.

11
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.812
The new store keeps payments the same way.

12
00:00:50.812 --> 00:00:54.606
Each machine holds one slice of the data, called a partition.

13
00:00:54.606 --> 00:00:55.601
That's an aisle.

14
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:58.428
Every payment has an address in two parts.

15
00:00:58.428 --> 00:01:02.854
The partition key says which partition, like the sign over the aisle.

16
00:01:02.854 --> 00:01:04.329
Here, it's the account.

17
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:08.586
The sort key says where inside it, like the order along the shelf.

18
00:01:08.586 --> 00:01:09.835
Here, it's the time.

19
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.083
So a question can go three ways.

20
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:16.236
Give the whole address, and the store finds one item: a key lookup.

21
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.120
Give the account and a range of times: one run of neighbours, a range read.

22
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.456
Anything else is a scan: read everything, keep a few.

23
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.470
Now the café owner's question.

24
00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:30.022
It names a shop, but the address starts with the account.

25
00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:32.577
So the store has to read every partition.

26
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.211
So, before you choose any key, write down every question the app will ask, and how often.

27
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.901
Each line is called an access pattern.

28
00:01:40.901 --> 00:01:41.953
The app has six.

29
00:01:41.953 --> 00:01:43.796
We'll check the first three.

30
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:47.113
AP1: an account's payments, newest first.

31
00:01:47.113 --> 00:01:49.284
AP2: one payment, by its id.

32
00:01:49.284 --> 00:01:53.704
AP3: a shop's payments with one status, in a time window.

33
00:01:53.704 --> 00:01:55.565
That's the café owner's.

34
00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.090
The rule: every pattern must be a key lookup or a range read.

35
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.025
Never a scan.

36
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.934
Check them against our table, filed by account, then time.

37
00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:07.187
Which can it answer without a scan?

38
00:02:07.187 --> 00:02:08.217
Pause and guess.

39
00:02:11.433 --> 00:02:13.943
AP1, account A-1001, newest first.

40
00:02:13.943 --> 00:02:18.150
Go to its partition, start at the end, and step back two.

41
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.733
A range read: read two, return two.

42
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:24.441
AP2 gives a payment id, which isn't in the address.

43
00:02:24.441 --> 00:02:28.120
So the store reads every item: read six, return one.

44
00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.667
In a real table, that's all of them.

45
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.580
AP3 names a shop: also a scan.

46
00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:35.385
Back to the supermarket.

47
00:02:35.385 --> 00:02:42.939
Shoppers keep asking for offers, so the shop adds a stand by the door: the same products, arranged a second way.

48
00:02:42.939 --> 00:02:45.435
Now every delivery stocks two places.

49
00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:47.503
Do the same with the data.

50
00:02:47.503 --> 00:02:50.309
Add a second copy, filed by payment id.

51
00:02:50.309 --> 00:02:54.912
AP2 now gives that copy's whole address: a key lookup, read one.

52
00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:59.669
And a copy for the café owner, filed by shop, then status, then time.

53
00:02:59.833 --> 00:03:07.067
Her question picks shop M-0042, jumps to the failed run, and trims it to this morning: read two, return two.

54
00:03:07.067 --> 00:03:10.215
Her answer arrives before her coffee gets cold.

55
00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.670
Here the supermarket picture breaks.

56
00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.806
A tin can only sit in one place.

57
00:03:14.806 --> 00:03:17.476
Data can be copied as often as you like.

58
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:27.079
DynamoDB, a database Amazon runs for its customers, calls this copy a global secondary index, and keeps it up to date for you.

59
00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:34.228
In Cassandra, a free database companies run on their own machines, it's another table, and your code writes it.

60
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.571
So the shop copy holds shop, status and time.

61
00:03:37.571 --> 00:03:41.095
Any question about those three is fast now, right?

62
00:03:41.095 --> 00:03:43.913
Try shop M-0042, any status, since nine.

63
00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:51.881
Items are sorted by status first, then time, so the ones after nine sit in two runs, with older ones in between.

64
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.127
Not one run, so not one read: the store reads all five items to keep three.

65
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.253
That's the no-gaps rule: the sort key is used from left to right.

66
00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.333
And a filter won't rescue it.

67
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:08.731
It runs after the read, and you pay for every item read, not every item returned.

68
00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:09.895
Now the bill.

69
00:04:09.895 --> 00:04:15.907
When payment pay-81741 arrived, it went into the table and into both copies.

70
00:04:15.907 --> 00:04:18.359
One payment, three item writes.

71
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.492
A copy nobody asks for is a shelf you stock at every delivery, for nobody.

72
00:04:23.492 --> 00:04:26.663
So add a copy only for a question on your list.

73
00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:33.179
And the list has a price: a new question next quarter can mean a new copy, or rewriting old items.

74
00:04:33.179 --> 00:04:37.193
In exchange, the questions on your list stay fast at any size.

75
00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:39.397
So why did the bigger store say no?

76
00:04:39.397 --> 00:04:43.405
Its address started with the account, and the question named a shop.

77
00:04:43.633 --> 00:04:48.913
The fix was a second copy, filed by shop, planned before the first payment arrived.

78
00:04:48.913 --> 00:04:51.903
Write the questions down, then choose the keys.

79
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:53.673
One quick check.

80
00:04:53.673 --> 00:04:56.091
All failed payments, at any shop.

81
00:04:56.091 --> 00:04:58.437
Key lookup, range read, or scan?

82
00:04:58.437 --> 00:04:59.609
Pause and think.

83
00:05:02.833 --> 00:05:04.195
A scan of everything.

84
00:05:04.195 --> 00:05:08.671
Status is never a partition key, so the store can't pick a partition.
